# Spectral Analysis of Cu–Al2O3 Composite Materials

## 1. Overview

Optical emission spectroscopy was used to investigate the plasma generated during laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) measurements of Cu–Al2O3 composite materials.

The recorded emission spectra were analyzed to identify characteristic atomic and ionic emission lines and investigate their spectral behavior.

## 2. Elemental Emission Line Identification

Elemental identification was performed by analyzing the recorded LIBS spectra and comparing the observed emission lines with reliable reference data, including the NIST Atomic Spectra Database.

For the Cu–Al2O3 composite sample containing 1.1 wt% Al2O3, the following emission lines were identified:

### Copper (Cu I)

| Emission line | Wavelength (nm) |
|---|---:|
| Cu I | 325.07 |
| Cu I | 465.44 |
| Cu I | 510.93 |
| Cu I | 515.67 |
| Cu I | 521.99 |
| Cu I | 578.57 |

### Aluminum (Al II)

| Emission line | Wavelength (nm) |
|---|---:|
| Al II | 327.66 |
| Al II | 386.48 |
| Al II | 402.76 |
| Al II | 459.03 |
| Al II | 744.52 |
| Al II | 747.24 |

Cu I represents neutral copper, whereas Al II represents singly ionized aluminum.

## 3. Spectral Line Selection Criteria

The observed emission lines were evaluated using the following criteria:

- Relatively high intensity within their respective spectral regions.
- Wavelength agreement within the specified interval of ±0.65.
- Signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) greater than 3 for the reported elemental data.

These criteria were used to support the reliability of the identified spectral features.

## 4. Relative Intensity Interpretation

Relative intensity was considered a qualitative characteristic describing the apparent strength of emission lines within a recorded spectrum.

Since the NIST database does not provide information about the origin and experimental conditions of the relative intensity values in the measured spectrum, these values were treated as qualitative indicators rather than direct quantitative measurements.

They were therefore used to describe the spectral appearance and relative prominence of emission features.

## 5. Research Focus

The spectral analysis focused on:

- Identification of copper and aluminum emission lines.
- Comparison of observed wavelengths with reference spectral data.
- Evaluation of spectral line intensity and signal-to-noise ratio.
- Investigation of plasma emission characteristics.
- Assessment of self-absorption effects in LIBS measurements.
- Supporting quantitative compositional analysis of Cu–Al2O3 composites.

## 6. Scope of This Repository

This repository documents the spectroscopic analysis and experimental research conducted as part of my MSc thesis:

"Laser-Induced Micro-Plasma Spectroscopy for Quality Control of Industrial Composites with Self-Absorption Correction."

The presented emission wavelengths and analysis criteria are based on the experimental research.

